        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">What Is an Exponential Function?</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          An <strong>exponential function</strong> is a fundamental mathematical relation in which the independent variable <em>x</em> appears in the exponent rather than in the base. Unlike linear functions that increase by adding a fixed constant with each step, or polynomial functions where a variable is raised to a fixed power, exponential functions grow or decay by multiplying by a constant factor for every unit increase in <em>x</em>.
        </p>
        <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 font-mono text-center text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          f(x) = a · bˣ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;or&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; f(x) = a · eᶜˣ
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          In these standard formulations:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 list-disc list-inside">
          <li><strong>x (The Exponent):</strong> The independent input variable, which can be any real number (positive, negative, fractional, or zero).</li>
          <li><strong>b (The Base):</strong> A strictly positive real constant (<em>b &gt; 0</em> and <em>b ≠ 1</em>) that governs the rate of multiplication per unit of <em>x</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>a (The Initial Value / Scale Factor):</strong> The coefficient equal to the vertical intercept <em>f(0)</em> when <em>x = 0</em>. It dictates the amplitude and sign of the function.</li>
          <li><strong>e (Euler's Constant):</strong> The mathematical constant <em>e ≈ 2.718281828...</em>, which represents continuous compounding growth.</li>
          <li><strong>c (Continuous Rate Constant):</strong> The proportional growth rate (when <em>c &gt; 0</em>) or decay rate (when <em>c &lt; 0</em>) in natural exponential models.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Standard Forms of Exponential Functions</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/50">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">1. Basic Form: f(x) = bˣ</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              The simplest exponential curve passing through (0, 1). For example, <em>f(x) = 2ˣ</em> doubles with each unit step of x, while <em>f(x) = (1/2)ˣ</em> halves with each step.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/50">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">2. Scaled Form: f(x) = a · bˣ</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              The most common model for practical scenarios. The multiplier <em>a</em> scales the curve vertically, setting the starting value <em>f(0) = a</em> at time zero.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/50">
