            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              When the base <em>b &gt; 1</em> (or continuous rate <em>c &gt; 0</em> for positive <em>a</em>), the function values surge upward at an accelerating rate as <em>x</em> increases.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              <li>As x ➔ +∞, f(x) ➔ +∞</li>
              <li>As x ➔ −∞, f(x) approaches the horizontal asymptote y = 0</li>
              <li>Real-world examples: Unconstrained bacterial cell division, compounding investments, viral spread.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50/50 p-5 dark:border-amber-900/50 dark:bg-amber-950/20">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-black text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-300 flex items-center gap-1.5">
              <span>📉</span> Exponential Decay (0 &lt; b &lt; 1 or c &lt; 0)
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              When the base is between zero and one (<em>0 &lt; b &lt; 1</em>) or continuous rate <em>c &lt; 0</em> (with positive <em>a</em>), the function diminishes rapidly toward zero.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              <li>As x ➔ +∞, f(x) asymptotically approaches y = 0</li>
              <li>As x ➔ −∞, f(x) ➔ +∞</li>
              <li>Real-world examples: Radioactive half-life, medication elimination from blood plasma, cooling of hot coffee.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Handling Special Mathematical Cases: Negative Y, Euler's e, and Equal Y</h2>
        <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            <strong>Can Exponential Functions Have Negative Y Values?</strong> Yes. In real analysis, while the base must satisfy <em>b &gt; 0</em>, the initial coefficient <em>a</em> can be negative. For instance, the function <em>f(x) = −2 · 3ˣ</em> produces negative values for every real input <em>x</em>. It represents an exponential growth curve reflected across the x-axis. However, because <em>bˣ &gt; 0</em> for all real <em>x</em>, both points must have the <em>same sign</em> (both positive or both negative). If <em>y₁</em> and <em>y₂</em> have opposite signs, no real exponential function can pass through both points.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What If y₁ = y₂? (b = 1):</strong> If the two points share the same y-value at different x-coordinates (such as (1, 5) and (4, 5)), then <em>y₁ / y₂ = 1</em>, which yields <em>b = 1</em>. Because <em>1ˣ = 1</em> for all real <em>x</em>, the function degenerates into the horizontal constant function <em>f(x) = a</em>. Our calculator correctly identifies and evaluates this as a constant function.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Euler's Number e Mode:</strong> When working with the natural base <em>e ≈ 2.718281828...</em> in the form <em>f(x) = a · eᶜˣ</em>, dividing the equations yields <em>y₁ / y₂ = e^(c(x₁ − x₂))</em>. Taking the natural logarithm (ln) of both sides allows direct calculation of continuous rate <em>c = ln(y₁ / y₂) / (x₁ − x₂)</em>.
          </p>
        </div>
